<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northern and Southern dynasties</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/420">420</a>–<a href="/years/589">589</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Northern and Southern dynasties was a period of political division in the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 420 to 589, following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> and the Eastern Jin dynasty. It began when Liu Yu seized the throne and founded the Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>; in the south the Liu Song was followed by the Southern Qi, Liang and Chen, while in the north the Northern Wei, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> ethnicity, gave way to the Eastern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a>. The age saw civil war, large-scale migration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a>. It ended when Emperor Wen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> unified China proper in 589.</p></div>
